{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>For the tribe of Judah, etc.</b> One needs to know why the tribe of Judah, the tribe of Simeon, and the tribe of Benjamin differ in that he did not mention 'prince' for them. And perhaps it is because, being adjacent to the mention of ״one prince, one prince,״ it is understood that they are princes; and from the third onward the mention of princely status was interrupted, and Scripture needed to state it for each one." "<b>And I saw</b> in the name of Rabbenu Nissim Gaon that for Judah it was not necessary, for Caleb is well known as a prince. Simeon was not fit for a prince because of the incident of Zimri. Benjamin — Elidad is Eldad, and he had already attained prophecy (Numbers 11:26–29), and it would be a demotion for him to be called prince — thus far." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say further, that he did not ascribe princely status to Caleb over Judah because of the honor of the kingship that would arise from him; and similarly for Benjamin, because from him begins the kingship in Israel — he is Saul the Benjaminite. And Simeon's reason is as the Gaon said."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:17:16.254246453Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}